        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" fontweight="bold">MODEL INPUT</paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">• Use the complete simultaneous simplified load-set row; independent maximum components are not a load case.</paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" fontweight="bold">SCOPE</paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">• Included: global wind-load derivation and transfer to the cap reference.</paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">• Separate: attachment design, bearing uplift, physical FE node assignment, and foundation capacity.</paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="14" spacingafter="7" spacingbefore="12" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True">DESIGN BASIS AND ASSUMPTIONS</paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="12" spacingafter="6" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True">CALCULATION STRATEGY</paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="10" spacingafter="8">
            The numbered strategy and blue calculation labels use the same seven steps. The fence itself uses the prescribed
            <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
            gross-surface load; wind-speed pressure equations remain only where needed for the bridge concrete and direct pier wind.
        </paragraph>
        <section name="FWStrategyStep01" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="5" spacingbefore="9" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" id="paragraphfwstrategy01">1. Code basis, reference point, and directions.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1" id="paragraphrefreturn0022">
                • Use
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage12;paragraphprojectcoderef12C002">FDOT SDG 10.3</link>
                to select the wind-design route for this concrete girder pedestrian bridge:
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage03;paragraphprojectcoderef03C002">SDG 2.4.1</link>
                together with LRFD Section 3.8.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Use the pier cap reference as the common point for all moments. T is across the bridge, L is along it, and V is upward.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingafter="9" level="1" fontweight="bold" textcolor="#1766A0">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyWork01;paragraphfwstrategywork01">Go to Step 1: Code basis, reference point, and directions</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <section name="FWStrategyStep02" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="5" spacingbefore="9" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" id="paragraphfwstrategy02">2. Fence geometry and tributary length.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Read the fence enclosure geometry from the Ped Bridge module and compute the vertical straight-leg height and cap-reference elevations.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Use half of each neighboring simply supported span to obtain the bridge length whose uniform wind load reaches this pier.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingafter="9" level="1" fontweight="bold" textcolor="#1766A0">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyWork02;paragraphfwstrategywork02">Go to Step 2: Fence geometry and tributary length</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <section name="FWStrategyStep03" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="5" spacingbefore="9" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" id="paragraphfwstrategy03">
                3. Prescribed
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                fence load.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">
                • Use the
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                pressure of 0.015 ksf on the gross fence surface, including openings; for global bridge transfer, apply it once to the wind-exposed projected face for a given wind direction.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Form one horizontal fence resultant and moment from that one-face projected load. Check the opposite wind direction by sign reversal; do not add a second simultaneous leeward pressure or a leeward reduction factor.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingafter="9" level="1" fontweight="bold" textcolor="#1766A0">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyWork03;paragraphfwstrategywork03">Go to Step 3: Prescribed LRFD 13.8.2 fence load</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <section name="FWStrategyStep04" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="5" spacingbefore="9" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" id="paragraphfwstrategy04">4. Simplified superstructure wind components.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Add the concrete superstructure wind to the prescribed fence resultant at the common cap reference and use the perpendicular-wind result as the transverse basis.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">
                • Check the
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage10;paragraphprojectcoderef10C002">LRFD 3.8.1.2.3a</link>
                span and height limits, then use 100% transverse and 25% longitudinal simultaneously instead of sweeping the plan-angle coefficient table.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingafter="9" level="1" fontweight="bold" textcolor="#1766A0">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyWork04;paragraphfwstrategywork04">Go to Step 4: Simplified superstructure wind components</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <section name="FWStrategyStep05" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="5" spacingbefore="9" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" id="paragraphfwstrategy05">5. Wind acting directly on the pier.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Use the FDOT/LRFD wind-speed pressure equation only for wind acting directly on the pier wall and cap.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Keep direct wall forces separate from the cap-reference bridge-and-fence rows until they are paired to the same wind direction.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingafter="9" level="1" fontweight="bold" textcolor="#1766A0">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyWork05;paragraphfwstrategywork05">Go to Step 5: Wind acting directly on the pier</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <section name="FWStrategyStep06" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="5" spacingbefore="9" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" id="paragraphfwstrategy06">6. Prepare the transfer to analysis nodes.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Preserve each concurrent resultant while moving loads to physical analysis nodes.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• If a force is applied away from the cap reference, carry the residual couple needed to preserve equilibrium.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingafter="9" level="1" fontweight="bold" textcolor="#1766A0">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyWork06;paragraphfwstrategywork06">Go to Step 6: Prepare the transfer to analysis nodes</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <section name="FWStrategyStep07" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="5" spacingbefore="9" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" id="paragraphfwstrategy07">7. Check the simplified method and complete the design handoff.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Check geometry, tributary length, resultant equilibrium, the simplified-method qualification, and the 100%/25% component relationships.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Hand off the simultaneous simplified superstructure/fence load set and the separate direct-pier wind components for FE analysis and the remaining design checks.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingafter="9" level="1" fontweight="bold" textcolor="#1766A0">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyWork07;paragraphfwstrategywork07">Go to Step 7: Check the simplified method and complete the design handoff</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <section name="FWCodeBasis" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="12" spacingafter="6" spacingbefore="10" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True">CODE REQUIREMENTS AND MODELING ASSUMPTIONS</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec01">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage01;paragraphprojectcoderef01C002">RFQ — Governing Regulations.</link>
                The contract defines the applicable edition by Department adoption at advertisement, with later editions documented in the Task Work Order. We will retain this journal’s stated
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage17;paragraphprojectcoderef17C002">LRFD 10th Edition (2024)</link>
                and January 2026 FDOT basis, and confirm them against that contract record. The listed Structures Manual modifications do not identify a replacement for
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage03;paragraphprojectcoderef03C002">SDG 2.4.1.A</link>
                .
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec02">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage12;paragraphprojectcoderef12C002">FDOT SDG 10.3.A–D</link>
                .
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage12;paragraphprojectcoderef12C002">SDG 10.3</link>
                supplements the pedestrian-bridge guide. Paragraph A uses the SDG wind-speed table for pedestrian bridges. Paragraph B routes truss-type bridges to LRFD LTS and other bridge types to
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage03;paragraphprojectcoderef03C002">SDG 2.4.1</link>
                plus LRFD BDS. We will use the latter route for this concrete girder bridge. Paragraphs C–D identify when special wind analysis or Department review is needed; the Standard Plans 550-012 fencing exception must be checked against the actual proposed fence.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec03">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage15;paragraphprojectcoderef15C002">LRFD 3.8.1.1.1</link>
                . The main bridge wind module uses exposed bridge area for the concrete superstructure. The fence in this worksheet is instead loaded by the specific
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                metal-fabric fence provision.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec04">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage04;paragraphprojectcoderef04C002">FDOT SDG 2.4.1.A</link>
                and
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage04;paragraphprojectcoderef04C002">Table 2.4.1-1</link>
                . The table replaces the LRFD wind-speed map for this purpose. Its Duval County value is 130 mph. We will use that design 3-second gust speed for Strength III. This is the specified design speed, not a claim that stronger winds are physically impossible.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec05">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage06;paragraphprojectcoderef06C002">LRFD 3.8.1.1.2</link>
                and
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage06;paragraphprojectcoderef06C002">Table 3.8.1.1.2-1</link>
                . The table gives 70 mph for Service I. We will calculate its pressure separately from the 130 mph Strength III pressure. The article also addresses higher speeds indicated by records or site-specific studies; a larger justified site criterion must be resolved in the design basis.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec06">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage05;paragraphprojectcoderef05C002">FDOT SDG 2.4.1.B</link>
                and
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage07;paragraphprojectcoderef07C002">LRFD 3.8.1.1.5</link>
                . FDOT requires Ground Surface Roughness Category C unless Category D applies. We will use the adopted upwind terrain category to select the exposure calculation; Category D applicability depends on the actual site and wind direction.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec07">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage05;paragraphprojectcoderef05C002">FDOT SDG 2.4.1.C</link>
                . Short code quote: “Use 0.85 for all load combinations and all structures”. We will use G = 0.85 for both bridge wind sets here. This FDOT provision replaces the LRFD gust table and definition; the accompanying commentary excludes ancillary sign, signal, lighting, ITS, and tolling supports from this wording.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec08">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage07;paragraphprojectcoderef07C002">LRFD Eq. 3.8.1.2.1-1</link>
                . The LRFD wind-pressure equation squares wind speed and multiplies by the unit constant, exposure-and-height factor, gust factor, and drag factor. In this worksheet it is used for direct pier wind; the concrete superstructure pressure is imported from the main bridge wind module. It is not used to generate a second fence pressure.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec09">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage08;paragraphprojectcoderef08C002">LRFD 3.8.1.2.1</link>
                and
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage08;paragraphprojectcoderef08C002">Eqs. 3.8.1.2.1-3</link>
                and
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage08;paragraphprojectcoderef08C002">3.8.1.2.1-4</link>
                . Strength III uses the height-dependent Category C or D equation. Service I uses K_Z = 1.0 under the definition following
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage07;paragraphprojectcoderef07C002">Eq. 3.8.1.2.1-1</link>
                . We will retain the 33 ft minimum height. The main wind module uses the fence crown as a common exposure height; that common-height choice is a project assumption. The LRFD height definitions remain the basis for checking its applicability.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec10">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage09;paragraphprojectcoderef09C002">LRFD Table 3.8.1.2.1-2</link>
                . The table gives drag values used for the bridge concrete and direct substructure wind calculations. The direct pier calculation uses the bridge-substructure value C_D = 1.6. No mesh or fence-member drag coefficient is needed for the
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                gross-surface fence load.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec11">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                . The specified chain-link/metal-fabric fence pressure is 0.015 ksf acting normal to the entire surface. For this global bridge-transfer worksheet, that prescribed load is applied once to the gross projected fence face exposed for a selected wind direction. The opposite wind direction is a separate signed case. No second simultaneous leeward 0.015 ksf pressure and no leeward reduction factor are introduced because
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                does not provide a separate windward/leeward pressure pair. This projected-resultant treatment is the adopted global model for the curved enclosure; local fence-member and attachment design remains separate.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec12">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage10;paragraphprojectcoderef10C002">LRFD 3.8.1.2.3a</link>
                . For usual girder and slab bridges having an individual span length not more than 150 ft and a maximum height not more than 33.0 ft above low ground or water level, the simplified wind components may be used in lieu of the plan-angle coefficient sweep: transverse equals 100 percent of the wind load for wind perpendicular to the bridge longitudinal axis, and longitudinal equals 25 percent of that transverse load; both act simultaneously. This worksheet checks the span and height limits live. For the height check, the bridge height is taken to the top of the concrete superstructure; the metal-fabric fence is loaded separately under
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                .
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec13">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage10;paragraphprojectcoderef10C002">LRFD 3.8.1.2.3b</link>
                and
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage10;paragraphprojectcoderef10C002">Commentary C3.8.1.2.3b</link>
                . For direct pier wind at a skew angle, the article resolves pressure normal to each exposed face and applies the resulting components with superstructure wind. Short commentary quote: “all wind forces should correspond to wind blowing from the same direction.” The present wall table instead retains full face-normal component values as an envelope. That envelope is not yet a set of direction-resolved wall loads paired to every bridge row.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec14">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage14;paragraphprojectcoderef14C002">LRFD Table 3.4.1-1</link>
                . The WS load factor is 1.0 for the Strength III and Service I combinations shown here. We will use each separately calculated wind set in its matching load combination, with the required companion loads. WS means wind on the structure; this table entry does not mean all companion loads or all design checks have been included in this wind worksheet.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec15">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage13;paragraphprojectcoderef13C002">FDOT SDG 10.10.B</link>
                and
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage13;paragraphprojectcoderef13C002">10.10.F</link>
                . For this concrete girder pedestrian bridge, the project engineer must design and detail the relevant attachment connections. Paragraph F calls for LRFD BDS and LRFD LTS in the listed attachment wind-design scope and refers back to
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage12;paragraphprojectcoderef12C002">SDG 10.3</link>
                . We will carry the global loads into the separate fence-member and attachment work; this worksheet does not perform those resistance checks.
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" id="paragraphfwcodec16">
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage11;paragraphprojectcoderef11C002">LRFD 3.8.2</link>
                . The global projected fence model in this worksheet carries no separate vertical fence component; this does not complete the bridge-deck uplift check. For the current pier, Strength III deck uplift and its concurrent perpendicular horizontal wind are addressed in C001/C004 and the W2 entry set. Service IV is not used. Deck uplift is not included in the simplified cap tables in this worksheet.
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <embed spacingbefore="8" spacingafter="12">
            <table name="FWContentBox1" capture="False" colwidths="1:520">
                <stylerule range="A1:A7" background="#F1F5FA" fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" border="Border(&quot;AllSides&quot;, &quot;Solid&quot;, 1, &quot;#7189A6&quot;);" />
                <stylerule range="A1" background="#DCE7F3" fontweight="bold" />
                <row>
                    <textcell capture="False">
                        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" spacingbefore="8" fontweight="bold">DESIGN BASIS QUICK REFERENCE</paragraph>
                    </textcell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <textcell capture="False">
                        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">
                            • GLOBAL BRIDGE WIND —
                            <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage12;paragraphprojectcoderef12C002">FDOT SDG 10.3</link>
                            routes this concrete girder pedestrian bridge to
                            <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage03;paragraphprojectcoderef03C002">SDG 2.4.1</link>
                            and LRFD Section 3.8.
                        </paragraph>
                    </textcell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <textcell capture="False">
                        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">
                            • FENCE —
                            <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                            supplies 0.015 ksf normal to the entire metal-fabric fence surface; this is the only fence pressure used here.
                        </paragraph>
                    </textcell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <textcell capture="False">
                        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">
                            • GEOMETRY — Semicircular enclosure geometry is retained, but the global
                            <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage16;paragraphprojectcoderef16C002">LRFD 13.8.2</link>
                            load is applied once to one wind-exposed projected face for each wind direction.
                        </paragraph>
                    </textcell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <textcell capture="False">
                        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">
                            • SIMPLIFIED SUPERSTRUCTURE WIND —
                            <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage10;paragraphprojectcoderef10C002">LRFD 3.8.1.2.3a</link>
                            permits 100% transverse plus 25% longitudinal simultaneously when the span and height limits are satisfied; this worksheet checks those limits live.
                        </paragraph>
                    </textcell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <textcell capture="False">
                        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">• DIRECT PIER WIND — Wind-speed pressure equations are retained only for the pier wall/cap and imported concrete wind.</paragraph>
                    </textcell>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <textcell capture="False">
                        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingafter="7" level="1">• LIMIT STATES — Strength III and Service I are kept as separate WS = 1.0 wind sets.</paragraph>
                    </textcell>
                </row>
            </table>
        </embed>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="14" spacingafter="7" spacingbefore="12" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True">CALCULATION EXECUTION — STEPS 1 THROUGH 7</paragraph>
        <section name="FWStrategyWork01" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="12" spacingbefore="12" spacingafter="4" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" textcolor="#1766A0" texthighlight="#EDF3F8" id="paragraphfwstrategywork01">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyStep01;paragraphfwstrategy01">STEP 1 OF 7 | Code basis, reference point, and directions</link>
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8">Establish the applicable code route, the pier cap reference, and the directions used by every later force and moment.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" textcolor="#43556B">
                Code basis: 
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage01;paragraphprojectcoderef01C002">RFQ — Governing Regulations</link>
                ; 
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage12;paragraphprojectcoderef12C002">FDOT SDG 10.3.A–D</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <embed spacingafter="12">
            <table name="FWSectionIntro1" title="Section introduction" capture="False" colwidths="1:90, 2:430">
                <stylerule range="A1:B3" background="#EDF3F8" fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="10" border="Border(&quot;AllSides&quot;, &quot;Solid&quot;, 1, &quot;#1766A0&quot;); Border(&quot;BetweenRows&quot;, &quot;Solid&quot;, 1, &quot;#CCD9E5&quot;);" />
                <stylerule range="A1:A3" fontweight="bold" />
                <row>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Objective</textvalue></c>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Establish design criteria and modeling assumptions.</textvalue></c>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Inputs</textvalue></c>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Bridge type; applicable code provisions; intended load application.</textvalue></c>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Output</textvalue></c>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Defined model, load cases, and scope boundaries.</textvalue></c>
                </row>
                <stylerule range="A1:B1" background="#F1F5FA" />
                <stylerule range="A2:B2" background="#FFF8E9" />
                <stylerule range="A3:B3" background="#EFF7F1" />
            </table>
        </embed>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="6" level="1">
            • This is a concrete girder pedestrian bridge.
            <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage12;paragraphprojectcoderef12C002">SDG 10.3.B</link>
            therefore routes global bridge wind to
            <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage03;paragraphprojectcoderef03C002">SDG 2.4.1</link>
            and LRFD BDS. The separate attachment scope remains identified in Step 7.
        </paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="6" level="1">• Use the cap reference as the common point for moments. T is across the bridge, L is along it, and V is upward. The later scalar load tables give component magnitudes; signed directions must be mapped to the analysis model.</paragraph>
        <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="6" level="1">• The fence and concrete loads reach this intermediate pier through the simply supported spans. Wind on the exposed pier faces acts directly on the pier and is calculated separately in Step 5.</paragraph>
        <section name="FWStrategyWork02" capture="False" hide="false">
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="12" spacingbefore="12" spacingafter="4" fontweight="bold" keepwithnext="True" textcolor="#1766A0" texthighlight="#EDF3F8" id="paragraphfwstrategywork02">
                <link address="#FenceWind.FWStrategyStep02;paragraphfwstrategy02">STEP 2 OF 7 | Fence geometry and tributary length</link>
            </paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="11" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8">Define the physical fence geometry and the tributary length used to transfer the prescribed fence load to the pier.</paragraph>
            <paragraph fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="9" spacingbefore="0" spacingafter="8" textcolor="#43556B">
                Code basis: 
                <link address="#CodeReferencesC002.CodePage15;paragraphprojectcoderef15C002">LRFD 3.8.1.1.1</link>
            </paragraph>
        </section>
        <embed spacingafter="12">
            <table name="FWSectionIntro2" title="Section introduction" capture="False" colwidths="1:90, 2:430">
                <stylerule range="A1:B3" background="#EDF3F8" fontfamily="Times New Roman" fontsize="10" border="Border(&quot;AllSides&quot;, &quot;Solid&quot;, 1, &quot;#1766A0&quot;); Border(&quot;BetweenRows&quot;, &quot;Solid&quot;, 1, &quot;#CCD9E5&quot;);" />
                <stylerule range="A1:A3" fontweight="bold" />
                <row>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Objective</textvalue></c>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Define the fence geometry and tributary length.</textvalue></c>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Inputs</textvalue></c>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Fence dimensions; cap-reference heights; neighboring span lengths.</textvalue></c>
                </row>
                <row>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Output</textvalue></c>
                    <c capture="False"><textvalue>Geometry variables and tributary length.</textvalue></c>
                </row>
                <stylerule range="A1:B1" background="#F1F5FA" />
                <stylerule range="A2:B2" background="#FFF8E9" />
                <stylerule range="A3:B3" background="#EFF7F1" />
            </table>
        </embed>
        <embed spacingafter="8">
